# Sponsor Design Doc — Context Reference

> Purpose: this file exists so future chat sessions don't need the original 81-page "Teaching AI Agents Final Design Document" PDF re-uploaded. It's a condensed but thorough summary of that document's actual content. For the *new* class project's own architecture and decisions (LangGraph, the demo scenario, etc.), see [project-architecture-plan.md](project-architecture-plan.md) in this same folder — that's the working doc, this is background/reference only.

## What this document is

The original PDF is the Senior Design I "Final Design Document" for a real, ongoing sponsor project: a student team (Haresh Palli, Kenneth Valladares, Sujan Katari, William Sharpe; sponsors Melissa Hills, Stewart Sutton) building an agentic AI system for the **United States Space Force (USSF)** to speed up acquisition research. **The sponsor already has their own production solution in progress** — the class project this context file supports is a separate, class-only demo/experiment inspired by this doc, not a continuation deliverable owed to the sponsor. See [project-architecture-plan.md](project-architecture-plan.md) section 1 for that framing.

## Problem statement (why this exists at all)

USSF acquisition timelines span years, which is too slow relative to how fast the space domain evolves and how fast adversaries move. Root causes named in the doc:
- Acquisition is governed by dense, constantly-updated documentation (DAU guidance, FAR/DFARS) that requires deep expertise to navigate.
- Staff manually comb through thousands of pages of PDFs for compliance — slow and error-prone.
- Commercial/public AI can't be used directly because much of the source material isn't public.
- No searchable knowledge repository exists — when experienced staff retire, their knowledge leaves with them.
- USSF is navigating a **40% reduction in O-1/O-2 acquisition staffing**, compounding all of the above.

## The sponsor's actual system architecture

**Core agent: `nanobot`** — not a chatbot. Described as an "action-oriented digital partner": it does file management, automated reporting, and can actually execute multi-step tasks (web research, drafting documents), not just answer questions.

**The two-machine split ("Tactical Hardware Deployment"):**
- **Mac Mini ("Shield")** — runs nanobot. Handles all external communication: receiving user requests, managing conversation state, deciding what actions to take, doing internet research. This is the exposed/untrusted-facing half of the system.
- **Mac Studio ("Brain")** — runs the actual LLM (Qwen3-32B, via LM Studio) and hosts the **Garrison API**. Never connects to the internet directly. Receives structured requests from the Mac Mini over a private point-to-point Ethernet link with a static IP schema, does the heavy inference, returns only processed text.
- This is a deliberate hardware-level air gap, not just a software boundary — the philosophy is that even if the Mac Mini (internet-facing) is compromised, the Mac Studio (holding the actual model and sensitive reasoning) is physically isolated from that blast radius.

**Garrison API** — the middleware between nanobot and the Edgerunner/Qwen models. Nanobot never talks to the model directly; everything goes through this API, which also centralizes logging/monitoring. Documented ports/endpoints in the original doc:
- Port 3000: Operator Web UI (browser, auth required)
- Port 8000: Garrison API itself (backend, JWT/cookie auth)
- Port 8080: raw llama-server (OpenAI/Anthropic-compatible, no auth)
- Endpoints include `/chat/completions`, `/history`, `/files`, `/collections` (RAG collections), `/embeddings`, `/images/generations`, `/audio/transcriptions`, `/audio/speech`.

**Edgerunner AI** — the third-party startup whose models (fine-tuned Qwen, trained on DAU documents) the sponsor's system relies on for RAG/document reasoning. This is the piece the new class project replaces with a self-hosted equivalent (Qwen via LM Studio directly) since the class team won't have access to Edgerunner's proprietary models/infrastructure.

## Why nanobot specifically (framework selection reasoning)

Three options were compared:
- **OpenClaw** — rejected: built for personal chatbot-style use, not real task execution; also had "numerous security concerns."
- **NanoClaw** — the *original* choice, later abandoned. Reason for abandoning: NanoClaw only supports **Anthropic's API format**, but the sponsor needed **OpenAI's API format** specifically because of tools the sponsor wanted to use in the acquisition workflow.
- **nanobot** — final choice. Supports task decomposition (breaking a request into steps: call APIs, search, organize output), gives fine-grained control over what the agent is allowed to do (important for Zero Trust), and supports the required OpenAI API format.

## Security architecture (Zero Trust, in depth)

- **Docker, ephemeral by design** — nanobot's web-research environment runs in hardened, non-persistent Docker containers. Every session starts from a clean state; containers are destroyed after each task, wiping cookies/cache/any session-based malware. Docker Swarm mentioned as a possible way to run multiple container copies for redundancy or throughput.
- **IP masking** — nanobot's outbound traffic is routed through rotating, non-attributable IP addresses so its web research can't be traced back to USSF infrastructure by an adversary watching for acquisition-intent signals.
- **Mediation layer** — a middleware gateway between nanobot and the public internet that strips identifying metadata (browser headers, fingerprints) and replaces it with a generic persona before any external request goes out.
- **Read/write access** — nanobot only gets write access to a scoped staging/cache directory for web content; nothing else. Principle of least privilege applied literally at the filesystem level.
- **Logging** — all nanobot actions and outputs are comprehensively logged, timestamped, and encrypted. Framed as a direct advantage over less secure alternatives (OpenClaw) and the backbone of the "Auditability" pillar.

## Disaster recovery

- **Vector store backup** — automated daily encrypted backups of the RAG vector database to a separate physical drive; recovery rolls back to last stable snapshot rather than re-processing the entire document library (which would take days).
- **LLM version control** — specific LLM weight versions and LM Studio configs are pinned once verified by the test plan; a failing new model version can be reverted to the last known-good local version.
- **Emergency handoff** — a physical/manual continuity plan if nanobot goes fully offline: hardware location maps, credential escrow (SSH/VPN keys), and a hard-copy index of what's in the vector database so officers can find source documents manually.
- **Fallback protocols** — on hardware failure, the system drops to "manual reference mode" (research tools disabled, direct links to DAU folders provided) rather than trying to stay up in a degraded/insecure state. On network interruption between the two Macs, all agent processes suspend and state is saved locally rather than lost.

## Test plan categories (from the doc — useful as a template for our own testing)

| Category | Pass criteria named in the doc |
|---|---|
| Unit Testing | ≥90% line coverage, no regressions; Pytest + Jest |
| Integration Testing | Successful end-to-end workflows; IP masking consistently shows non-USSF address |
| Red-Team Testing | Agent cannot escalate privileges, cannot leak system prompts, stays sandboxed regardless of adversarial input (container escape, prompt injection, data exfiltration attempts) |
| Latency Testing | ≥50% reduction in document drafting time vs. manual; System Usability Scale score >80 |
| Hallucination Testing | <5% hallucination rate; 100% correct citation of real regulatory paragraph numbers (uses RAGAS framework) |
| Persona Drift Testing | ≥95% consistency score across longitudinal tests; zero instances of dropping character over extended use |

## Where our project's core idea actually comes from

Buried in the original doc's "What's Next" section (future work the sponsor's team hadn't built yet) is a **Researcher + Auditor Critic Loop**: a Researcher persona optimized for high-recall data gathering, paired with a separate Auditor persona optimized for high-precision validation. The Auditor red-teams the Researcher's output for hallucinations, stale citations, and logical inconsistencies, sends it back for revision if it finds a problem, and only a human-in-the-loop reviewer sees the output once both agents reach consensus. **That pattern, still just a described concept in the sponsor's doc, is what our class project actually builds and makes literal.**

The same "What's Next" section also names a longer-term vision of distilling the model down to a much smaller size so it can run on ruggedized/offline field hardware with no network dependency — explicitly for survivability in contested/degraded environments where a live link to a large model isn't guaranteed. That's the textual basis for our own local/edge deployment stance below — it's not us dodging a "should be cloud-hosted" expectation, it's the same design philosophy the sponsor's own team already committed to on paper, just realized now instead of left as a someday-idea.

## What the new class project intentionally does differently — and how, concretely

**1. The Critic Loop becomes an actual LangGraph state machine, not a described pattern.** Two graphs, not one:
- **IngestionGraph** (parse → chunk → embed → store) — triggered whenever a document shows up. In our design, that's triggered by dropping a PDF into the Slack channel, not through a dashboard upload feature (the dashboard is read-only, purely a mirror of Postgres, never an input surface).
- **ConversationGraph** — triggered fresh on every single Slack message. Nodes: **Route** (decides if this request needs live web research or can be answered from documents alone) → **Researcher** (nanobot — live web search, sandboxed) → **Analyst** (Qwen — drafts an answer grounded in retrieved document chunks + Researcher's findings) → **Auditor** (Qwen, a genuinely separate node/call with its own prompt — checks the Analyst's draft for gaps, hallucinations, stale citations) → **HumanReview** (a LangGraph `interrupt()` that pauses the graph and posts to Slack for approval) → **Notify** (delivers the final answer, logs the run).
- The Auditor's rejection loops back to Researcher/Analyst for revision, capped at a small number of retries (a loop guard) so an unlucky live-model disagreement can't spin forever — important since this runs live in front of a class.
- **HumanReview is threshold-gated, not "every run" or "never."** The Auditor returns a `risk_score` alongside its verdict; a configurable threshold decides whether a given approved answer needs a human to sign off, or auto-approves straight to Notify. A global override toggle exists to force review on every run when that's useful (e.g., during a live demo, so the audience always sees the approval gate fire).

**2. Three layers, not a flat list of parts.** Slack and the dashboard are *interfaces* — they move messages/state in and out but make no decisions. LangGraph is the *one* orchestrator — it's the only thing that decides what happens next. nanobot and the Qwen model are *worker agents* — opaque nodes the orchestrator calls, the same relationship nanobot originally had to the Garrison API/Edgerunner models, just self-hosted now.

**3. Edgerunner + Garrison API is replaced with a self-hosted equivalent** — Qwen via LM Studio directly, called by LangGraph the way nanobot used to call Garrison — since the class team has no access to Edgerunner's actual proprietary infrastructure.

**4. Deployment is local-first by design, matching the sponsor's own edge-hardware ideal, not despite it.** Primary target: **one local machine** running the entire stack (FastAPI/LangGraph, nanobot's sandbox, LM Studio, Postgres+pgvector, PDF store) — realistic given the team is building on personal devices, not the sponsor's actual hardware. Backup: the sponsor's original two-Mac split (Mac Mini executing, Mac Studio doing inference), used only if that physical hardware becomes available. **There is no cloud option at either tier.** When a class rubric expects "where's this deployed" to mean "the cloud," the honest and correct answer here is the opposite: this system's entire premise is that acquisition data and inference never leave controlled, local hardware, and the sponsor's own doc already names small/edge-deployed models as the intended end state — building this locally is executing that vision, not falling short of a cloud-native one.

**5. Model tiering accounts for the team actually building this on personal laptops.** Not everyone has hardware to run something like Qwen3-32B. Dev/demo default is a smaller quantized model from the same family (e.g. `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`, 4-bit GGUF via LM Studio) that runs on a typical modern laptop; the larger model stays aspirational for if stronger hardware (the sponsor's real Mac Studio, or a teammate's GPU) becomes available. Because the Auditor's structured `{is_valid, risk_score, issues}` output has to stay reliably parseable for any of the above routing to work, smaller/quantized models need constrained/grammar-based JSON decoding rather than hoping they free-form valid JSON.

**6. The demo scenario is grounded in real material, not a fabricated case study.** The class team's actual ~28 DAU guideline PDFs (DoD Cloud Acquisition Guidebook, Agile Software Acquisition Guidebook, etc.) ground the RAG/onboarding half of a demo; nanobot's live web search against real public sources (SAM.gov, USAspending.gov) covers the market-research half — using a real, current example (the Air Force/Space Force **Cloud One** program, awarded to **SAIC**, ~$382.7M in March 2026). The Auditor's catch in the demo (flagging that a draft recommendation doesn't address vendor lock-in/hidden-cost risk) is grounded in a risk the team's actual Cloud Acquisition Guidebook names — not a staged/fake conflict.

